  "query_preview": "..Radar and satellite imagery shows a few light \nshowers near the White mountains in portions of eastern Gila \ncounty early this afternoon, with additional showers and \nthunderstorms developing further south in southeastern AZ. \nOtherwise, breezy southwest winds with gusts up to 35 mph, \nespecially in western Arizona, and dry pattern extends across \nnorthern AZ as the trough continues to be centered over the \nPacific Northwest. Hi-res models still to show the potential for \novernight convection tonight into early Friday morning in Gila, \nsouthern and central Apache and Navajo, and southeastern Yavapai \ncounties, but this will be highly dependent on how showers and \nthunderstorms activity develops in southeastern AZ, so the chance \nfor showers for showers and thunderstorms for most areas were kept\naround 40% or less. \n\nStill expecting a gradual increase in shower and thunderstorm \ncoverage Friday and into the weekend as moisture associated with \nHurricane Marie is siphoned by the large trough and lifted\nnortheastward toward northwestern AZ, and a strengthening of the \nridge in the central US continues to support southerly flow in \nsoutheast AZ and pushing moisture northward into eastern AZ. The \nbiggest challenges however, are: 1) Timing of this moisture being \nsiphoned and being pushed into AZ. 2) How much moisture will be \nsiphoned and where the greatest concentration will set up. 3) What\nkind synoptic profile will be in place. For the moisture, models \nshow a gradual increase in moisture Friday and Saturday and have \ndelayed the bulk of the moisture to Sunday and have shifted it \nnortheastward into NV, UT, and far NW AZ. This is based on \nMarie's current track and speed and when the peak interaction \nbetween Marie and the trough will occur. For how much moisture, a\nPWAT around 0.92 inches is the 90th percentile of PWAT values for\nFlagstaff this time of year and the NBM shows a 30-40% chance\nexceeding this PWAT value by Saturday and a 50-65% chance Sunday,\nthen decreasing to around 40-50% by Monday evening. For the \nsynoptic profile, forecast ensembles have a the strongest 500 mb \nwinds setting up over NV and UT, with 25-35 kts winds over NW AZ \nand decreasing to 15-25 kts over eastern AZ as the large trough \nmoves northeast across the Pacific NW. The placement of the \nmoisture and synoptic pattern currently places the greatest risk \nfor storms and flooding somewhere around or north of the NV, UT, \nAZ border, but this pattern may shift over the next few days, and \ndoes not mean showers and thunderstorms will not develop across \ncentral or eastern AZ as dirnual-monsoonal showers and \nthunderstorms along the higher terrain are possible, especially \nif the tropical moisture does not create a mid or upper-level \ncloud deck over the state during the daytime hours. For now, the \ngreatest potential for showers and thunderstorms on Sunday will be\nnear and north of the Kaibab Plateau and the White Mountains, \nwith day time temperatures staying near or slightly below normal \ninto early next week.",
